You are trying to solve a problem which does not exist. Of course your
construction is ungrammatical, so it does not mean anything. I
think you are proposing to change the grammar.

If I understood correctly, what you are trying to accomplish is to turn
a cmevla into a brivla.

Meet =93me=94. It turns the following sumti into a selbri. This is a more
general way to do it, but it still works.

Please read the part about =93me=94 in the CLL.
<http://dag.github.io/cll/5/10/>

It allows for constructions like this:

jbo: =93.i ko'a me la .uuZIT.=94
eng: =93He/she/it is Wuzzy.=94

jbo: =93.i ko'e me la tsani=94
eng: =93He/she/it is Sky.=94

That=92s short, simple, nice, and already part of the language.

Compare for:
jbo: =93.i ko'i tsani=94
eng: =93He/she/it is an expanse of sky at some place.=94

=93me=94 also allows for more complex constructions, see the reference
grammar.

> Another solution (in a fictional lojbophone world, at least) would be
> not to use me'evla at all.
This is not a solution because there is no problem. So it would strip
Lojban of a very good feature for absolutely no benefit. :O

In case I understood you totally wrong, please correct me. Honestly, I
can only guess from your posting what you actually wanted to
express. ;-) Your posting was rather vague.

